text.en:
and batta “he cut”, in [the sense of] cutting; and give a ḍamma to namma “he told tales”, together with / intransitivity, in umrur bihi “pass by him!”, and jalla [when it is] like jalā “he emigrated”,
habbat “[the wind] blew”, dharrat “[the sun] rose”, ajja “[the fire] blazed”, karra “he turned back [to attack]”, hamma bihi “he set his mind on it”, / ʿamma “it was general”, zamma “he raised [his head]”, saḥḥa “it poured”, malla that is dhamalā “he went at a brisk pace”,
alla in [the sense of] glittering and of crying out, shakka, abba “he made ready to go” and shadda / that is ʿadā “he ran”, shaqqa, khashsha and ghalla that is dakhalā “he entered”;

Faʿl is the rule for the transitive, and fuʿūl for what is other / than it, except a verb of sound: for this fuʿāl is plain;
and what is on faʿila, its maṣdar deserves, / if it is not transitive, to be faʿal;
and take faʿāla or fuʿūla as the rule for faʿulta, / as al-shajāʿati “courage” and what runs on sahulā “it was easy”.

Whoever joins tafaʿʿala with tifiʿʿāl, and / faʿʿala with al-fiʿʿāl, praise him for what he did;
and tafʿāl may be brought for faʿʿala, in / the multiplying of an act, as tasyārin “much travelling”; and made
for the triliteral is fiʿʿīlā, for intensity; / and from tafāʿul too it may be seen as a substitute.

What has a weak ʿayn: the ifʿāl from it and the istifʿāl / [take] the tāʾ, and compensation by it comes about
for what was removed; and if it is joined to other than these two, / a single time of the one who acted is made clear by it;
and the single time of the maṣdar to which it [the tāʾ] is necessary / appears, for one who understands, by mentioning wāḥida “one”.

From the noun of what is abundant, the name of the land is mafʿala, / like masbaʿatin “a land of many lions”, and the added [letter] is cut off
from the augmented, as mafʿātin “a land of many vipers”; and mufʿila / and afʿalat have been allowed by them in this;
other than the triliteral is barred from this pattern, / and sometimes a rare [form] of it has come and been accepted.

Like mifʿal, and like mifʿāl and mifʿala, / from the triliteral form the name of what [the act] is done with.
Irregular are al-muduqqu “the pestle”, musʿuṭun “snuff-box”, mukḥulatun “kohl-pot”, / mudʹhunun “oil-flask”, munṣulun “sword” and what comes from nakhalā “he sifted”;
and whoever means [by them] an instrument may / give them a kasra, and no heed is paid to one who blames [him].

and upon his radiant family and his noble companions, and whoever / followed them on the path of noble deeds.
I ask God, of the garments of His mercy, / a fair covering that wraps the slips;
and that He make easy for me an effort by which I shall be / rejoicing and glad, not frowning and afraid.
